{
  "cards": [
    {
      "front": "In an ideal DC resistive circuit, what equation relates voltage $V$, current $I$, and resistance $R$, and what are the units of each quantity?",
      "back": "Ohm's law is $V = IR$. Voltage $V$ is measured in volts (V), current $I$ in amperes (A), and resistance $R$ in ohms ($\\Omega$)."
    ,
      "topic": "Ohm's law"
    },
    {
      "front": "Two resistors of $2\\,\\Omega$ and $4\\,\\Omega$ are connected in series to a $12\\,\\text{V}$ source. Use Ohm's law to find the circuit current and the voltage drop across each resistor.",
      "back": "In series, the same current flows through both resistors and the equivalent resistance is $R_{eq}=2+4=6\\,\\Omega$. The current is $I=V/R_{eq}=12/6=2\\,\\text{A}$. The voltage drops are $V_1=IR_1=(2)(2)=4\\,\\text{V}$ and $V_2=IR_2=(2)(4)=8\\,\\text{V}$, which sum to the source voltage."
    ,
      "topic": "Ohm's law"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "cards": [
    {
      "front": "For resistors connected in series in an ideal DC resistive circuit, what relationship holds for the current through each resistor, and how is the equivalent resistance $R_{\\text{eq}}$ calculated from the individual resistances?",
      "back": "The same current flows through every resistor in the series path. The equivalent resistance is the sum of the individual resistances: $R_{\\text{eq}} = R_1 + R_2 + \\cdots + R_n$."
    ,
      "topic": "Series resistor circuits"
    },
    {
      "front": "Two resistors, $R_1 = 2\\,\\Omega$ and $R_2 = 4\\,\\Omega$, are connected in series across an ideal $12\\,\\text{V}$ DC source. What is the circuit current, and what are the voltage drops across $R_1$ and $R_2$?",
      "back": "First find $R_{\\text{eq}} = 2\\,\\Omega + 4\\,\\Omega = 6\\,\\Omega$. The current is $I = V/R_{\\text{eq}} = 12\\,\\text{V}/6\\,\\Omega = 2\\,\\text{A}$. Since the same current flows through both resistors, $V_1 = IR_1 = (2\\,\\text{A})(2\\,\\Omega) = 4\\,\\text{V}$ and $V_2 = IR_2 = (2\\,\\text{A})(4\\,\\Omega) = 8\\,\\text{V}$. The drops sum to the source voltage: $4\\,\\text{V} + 8\\,\\text{V} = 12\\,\\text{V}$."
    ,
      "topic": "Series resistor circuits"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "cards": [
    {
      "front": "For resistors connected in parallel, what equation gives the equivalent resistance in terms of the branch resistances?",
      "back": "The reciprocal of the equivalent resistance equals the sum of the reciprocals of the branch resistances: $1/R_{eq} = \\sum_i 1/R_i$. For two $6\\ \\Omega$ resistors, $1/R_{eq} = 1/6 + 1/6 = 1/3$, so $R_{eq} = 3\\ \\Omega$."
    ,
      "topic": "Parallel resistor circuits"
    },
    {
      "front": "In a parallel circuit with two $6\\ \\Omega$ resistors connected across a $12\\ \\text{V}$ supply, what current flows through each branch and what is the total current?",
      "back": "Each parallel branch has the same voltage, $V = 12\\ \\text{V}$. Using $I = V/R$, each branch current is $I = 12/6 = 2\\ \\text{A}$. The total current is the sum of the branch currents: $I_{\\text{total}} = 2 + 2 = 4\\ \\text{A}$."
      ,
      "topic": "Parallel resistor circuits"
    }
  ]
}

